# Active Directory Security Groups

## Overview
Security Groups in Active Directory are used to organize users and computers that require similar access permissions.

Instead of assigning permissions directly to individual users, administrators assign permissions to groups. Users who are members of those groups automatically inherit the permissions assigned to the group.

This makes managing access easier, more consistent, and more secure.

## Purpose
In this lab, security groups were created to represent different job roles within an organization.

Security groups allow administrators to:

- Manage permissions efficiently
- Apply access based on job responsibilities
- Reduce the need to configure users individually
- Follow the principle of least privilege

## Security Groups Created
The following security groups were created:

| Group Name | Purpose |

| IT_Admins | Provides administrative access for IT personnel |
| Help_Desk | Represents technical support personnel |
| HR | Represents Human Resources users |
| Finance | Represents Finance department users |

## Group Membership
Users were assigned to security groups based on their department and role.


## Creating Security Groups
Security groups were created using Active Directory Users and Computers.

Steps performed:

1. Opened Active Directory Users and Computers.
2. Selected the appropriate Organizational Unit.
3. Right-clicked and selected New → Group.
4. Entered the group name.
5. Selected Global scope.
6. Selected Security as the group type.
7. Added users as group members.

## Security Group Types
For this lab, Global Security Groups were used.

Global groups are commonly used to organize users based on job roles or departments. Permissions can then be assigned to these groups to control access to resources.

## Real-World Example
In an enterprise environment:

- A Finance group may receive access to financial documents.
- A Help Desk group may receive permissions to reset user passwords.
- IT Administrators may receive elevated administrative privileges.

Administrators manage access by changing group membership instead of changing permissions for each user individually.

## Verification
The security groups were verified through Active Directory Users and Computers by confirming that:

- Groups were created successfully.
- Users were added as members.
- Groups were located in the correct Organizational Units.

